Windows itself — UI Automation, SAPI, SendInput — is used through the operating
system and nothing of it is redistributed here.

**No dependency of this project is under a copyleft or a proprietary licence, and
that is a rule rather than an accident.** native/argmind-rules/tests/licences.rs
holds it: every dependency named in a Cargo.toml under native/ must appear in
docs/licences.toml with a licence from a permissive allowlist, so adding one that
does not fails the suite rather than being noticed later. It checks the other
direction too — that nothing recorded has stopped being a dependency — because a
record that keeps naming what was removed reads as thorough while being wrong,
which is exactly what this file did until 2026-09-14. The rule exists
because the alternative is remembering, and this project's standard is that a
rule nothing enforces is a rule nobody keeps.

One consequence is recorded here because it decides an engineering choice rather
than a legal one: **redistributing NVIDIA's CUDA runtime libraries is governed by
NVIDIA's licence and not by an open-source one**, so a GPU build that has to ship
cuBLAS or cuDNN is out. Where GPU acceleration is wanted, the Vulkan path is the
one that redistributes nothing proprietary.

This file lists licences as the projects themselves declare them. It is not legal
advice.
